# Foundation

The foundation family is the one type the rest of the actuarial surface is built on: [`Operator`](foundation/Operator.md), a named callable whose argument and result types are fixed at compile time. Every reusable calculation in the library (a valuation, a loss transform, a pricing run, a reserve) is an `Operator` with a domain alias, so they share one lifecycle: build once with the assumptions bound, call many times with the data.

## What This Family Owns

| You want | Use |
|---|---|
| To know what a library operator is | [`Operator`](foundation/Operator.md) |
| A calculation of your own with the same contract | [`make_operator`](foundation/make_operator.md) |
| To test whether an operator is usable | [`defined`](foundation/Operator/defined.md), [`operator bool`](foundation/Operator/operator_bool.md) |
| To label or log an operator | [`key`](foundation/Operator/key.md) |
| To run it | [`operator()`](foundation/Operator/operator_call.md) |

Use the domain alias when one exists: `Valuation`, `LossTransform`, `LossMeasure`, `Pricing`, `Credibility` and `Reserving` say more than `Operator<...>`. Reach for `make_operator` only for a rule the library does not ship; the result plugs in wherever the alias is expected. The numerical work is not done by the operator but by the `Series` kernels the callable uses.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1: a rule of your own

`make_operator` wraps a lambda; the operator reports its key and is called like the lambda.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const auto square = actuarial::make_operator<double(double)>("square", [](double x) { return x * x; });
  out.writeln(square.key(), square(4.0));
}
```

### Example 2: the same contract on a Series

A rule on whole vectors, and a library operator beside it.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const auto cap = actuarial::make_operator<pluss::Series(const pluss::Series &)>("cap_500", [](const pluss::Series &x) { return x.clamp_max(500.0); });
  const pluss::Series losses = pluss::make_series({100.0, 700.0, 450.0, 2000.0});
  out.writeln(cap.key(), cap(losses).items<double>());
  out.writeln(actuarial::operators::limit(500.0).key(), actuarial::operators::limit(500.0)(losses).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 3: an empty operator

An operator that was never set is reported by `defined` and refuses to run.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const auto report = [&](const auto &call) {
    try {
      call();
    } catch (const std::exception &e) {
      std::string text(e.what());
      if (const auto cut = text.find("Triggered"); cut != std::string::npos) text.erase(cut);
      while (!text.empty() && text.back() == '\n') text.pop_back();
      out.writeln(text);
    }
  };
  const actuarial::Operator<double(double)> none;
  out.writeln("defined:", none.defined());
  report([&] { (void)none(1.0); });
}
```

